# `.field` on an optional — `xs.max { |b| b.id }.id` — used to pass the
# checker and then break three different ways at run time: a crash on BEAM,
# "Undefined method: id for Optional<M.Book>" in the interpreter, and (worst,
# on 0.4.0-beta.2 BEAM) the whole record silently interpolated where the
# field was asked for. A plain record literal does not coerce to `X?`, so
# chaining off optional-returning methods is the spelling users reach for —
# the checker has to say no before the backends disagree about it
# (kexhq/kex#294). The message's suggested spelling is the one that runs.
module M do
  record Book do
    id : Integer
  end
end

main do
  let books = [M.Book { id: 3 }, M.Book { id: 1 }]
  IO.printLine("nonempty: ${books.max { |b| b.id }.id.or(0)}")
end